.TH "CREATURE-AUDIO-ROUTER" "1" "2026-10-09" "Creature Workshop" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
creature\-audio\-router \- route Creature Workshop audio to HDMI speakers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B creature\-audio\-router
.RB [ \-\-config " PATH" ]
.RB [ \-\-check\-config ]
.br
.B creature\-audio\-router
.RB { \-\-list\-sound\-devices " | " \-\-version " | " \-\-help }
.SH DESCRIPTION
Receives mono Opus/RTP bird voices and background music from the Creature
Server and mixes them into one native audio output. Linux uses ALSA;
macOS uses CoreAudio. The output supports one through eight named channels
at 48 kHz with signed 16-bit PCM.
.PP
Run one process per physical HDMI device. Two named systemd instances can
use the Raspberry Pi 5's two HDMI outputs. Each process decodes an input
once, then distributes it through the configured routes and optional
software bass crossover.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-config PATH
Read the JSON configuration from PATH. Default: /etc/creature/audio-router.json.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Validate configuration and exit without opening audio devices or sockets. This does not verify interface availability, device capabilities, permissions, or clock synchronization.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-sound\-devices
List native audio outputs and exit. Use an exact output name in output.deviceName.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the product version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration is a JSON object. Unknown fields, invalid ranges, duplicate
inputs or routes, and references to unsubscribed inputs are rejected.
Comments and trailing commas are not supported.
.SS Network and logging
.TP
.B networkInterface
Required interface name with one IPv4 address, or an explicit local IPv4 address. The interface name is resolved at startup.
.TP
.B rtpPort
RTP UDP port, 1 through 65534. Default: 5004. RTCP uses the adjacent port, normally 5005.
.TP
.B requireRtcp
Require usable BGM RTCP timing before playback. Default: true. Setting false permits arrival-time fallback for an entire source generation.
.TP
.B logLevel
One of trace, debug, info, warn, error, critical, or off. Default: info. Logs are written to standard output for capture by the journal.
.SS Output
The required output object contains deviceName and channels.
.TP
.B deviceName
Exact native output name. Startup fails if the selected device cannot be opened.
.TP
.B channels
Ordered array of speaker names: front_left, front_right, rear_left, rear_right, center, lfe, side_left, and side_right. The separate mono name is valid only for a single-channel output. Names must be unique. ALSA must expose a verifiable multichannel speaker map.
.TP
.B commonPlayoutDelayMs
Shared presentation delay, 20 through 500 ms. Default: 20. Use the same value on synchronized routers and controllers.
.TP
.B audioDeviceCompensationMs
Per-device correction, -100 through +100 ms. Default: 0. Positive values enqueue earlier to account for additional output latency. The common delay must leave at least 15 ms plus any positive compensation; measured native pipeline latency may require more.
.TP
.B limiterCeilingDb
Final PCM clamp ceiling, -90 through 0 dB. Default: -1. Provide mixing headroom with input and route gains.
.TP
.B outputVolumePercent
Optional ALSA hardware playback volume, 0 through 100. Omit to leave hardware volume unchanged.
.TP
.B alsaMixerCard
ALSA mixer card for optional hardware volume. Default: default.
.TP
.B alsaMixerElement
Optional ALSA playback-volume element. When omitted, common playback elements are preferred.
.SS Inputs and routes
The required inputs array contains objects with channel and optional gainDb.
Bird channels are 1 through 16; channel 17 is BGM and must be subscribed as
the timing master, even if it has no audible route. Each input joins
239.19.63.<channel>. Input gain defaults to 0 dB.
.PP
The required routes array contains objects with input (a subscribed channel
number), output (a configured speaker name), and optional gainDb (default 0).
Input gain applies before fanout; route gain trims an individual destination.
All gains accept -90 through +12 dB. Unrouted outputs remain silent.
.SS Optional bass crossover
The bass object selects an output named lfe, a crossoverHz value from 20
through 200 (default 80), gainDb (default 0), and invertPolarity (default false).
Its required inputs array contains source channel and optional gainDb objects.
Each source contributes once, independent of ordinary route fanout.
.PP
Optional highPassOutputs names the full-range destinations receiving
complementary high-pass filtering. The filters use a fourth-order
Linkwitz-Riley crossover. Without highPassOutputs, ordinary routes stay
full range. The bass output cannot also receive an ordinary route.
.SH SYNCHRONIZATION
Keep the server and receiving hosts' system clocks synchronized, normally
through the workshop PTP deployment. RTCP maps the server media timeline
to wall time; it does not synchronize host clocks.
.PP
BGM establishes the presentation timeline. A bird joins after its matching
RTCP report has the same CNAME and a compatible RTP/wall-clock relationship.
A missing or incompatible bird remains silent independently of BGM.
With requireRtcp enabled, unusable BGM timing keeps the output silent.
.PP
The audio device remains running through idle periods. Measure physical
HDMI/AVR latency before setting compensation. RTCP aligns session starts;
continuous correction of independent DAC clock drift is not implemented.
.SH SERVICES
The default service uses /etc/creature/audio-router.json. A named instance
creature-audio-router@NAME.service uses /etc/creature/audio-router/NAME.json.
Use one owner per physical audio device.
.PP
Services are disabled on first installation. They run as dynamic users with
membership in audio. Configuration files must be readable by those users.
After editing a file, validate it and restart its owning service.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/creature/audio\-router.json
Default service configuration; a Debian conffile preserved across upgrades.
.TP
.B /etc/creature/audio\-router/NAME.json
Site configuration for a named instance.
.TP
.B /usr/share/doc/creature\-audio\-router/examples/audio\-router\-bass\-example.json
Complete software bass configuration example.
.TP
.B /usr/share/doc/creature\-audio\-router/docs/audio\-router\-setup.md
Installation, speaker assignment, calibration, and troubleshooting manual.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate the default configuration:
.PP
.nf
creature-audio-router --check-config
.fi
.PP
Start the configured default service:
.PP
.nf
sudo systemctl enable --now creature-audio-router.service
journalctl -u creature-audio-router.service -f
.fi
.PP
Validate and start a configured HDMI instance:
.PP
.nf
creature-audio-router --check-config \e
    --config /etc/creature/audio-router/hdmi0.json
sudo systemctl enable --now creature-audio-router@hdmi0.service
.fi
.SH SIGNALS
SIGINT and SIGTERM request orderly shutdown. Configuration changes require
a restart; there is no live configuration reload.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 indicates successful validation, informational output, or orderly shutdown.
1 indicates invalid arguments/configuration or a startup/runtime failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR creature-server (1),
.BR speaker-test (1),
.BR aplay (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR journalctl (1)
